{
  "concept": "The distance to a boat can be recovered purely from geometry — either from a single observer's known height above the water using the dip angle to the horizon and to the boat, or by triangulating from two observers separated by a known baseline — and this is the same geometric principle that underlies stereo depth from parallax.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "coastal cliff / terrain silhouette filled ochre-brown, occupying the lower-left of the scene",
    "water surface filled turquoise, meeting the terrain at a curved shoreline",
    "horizontal horizon line across the scene where water meets sky",
    "small sailboat icon (white hull and sails, dark outline) floating on the water",
    "observer marker (circle with sight-line vertex) at the top of the cliff (left method)",
    "second observer marker lower on the cliff slope (right method)",
    "vertical height line from the observer down to the water's edge, labeled 'h', with a gray right-angle marker at its foot",
    "baseline segment along the slope between the two observers, labeled 't'",
    "sight line from observer(s) to the horizon",
    "sight line from observer(s) to the boat",
    "angle arc labeled 'α' between the horizon sight line and the boat sight line (at the top observer)",
    "angle arc labeled 'β' at the lower observer (right method)",
    "gray right-angle marker where the lower sight line meets the baseline (right method)",
    "label 'd?' on the water along the boat-to-shore direction, the quantity to be estimated"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "methodView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Method",
      "options": ["Single observer (height + horizon)", "Two observers (triangulation)"],
      "default": "Single observer (height + horizon)",
      "teaches": "The same scene supports two different geometric routes to distance: one observer exploiting a known height and the horizon as a reference line, versus two observers exploiting a known baseline — the second is the direct precursor of stereo vision"
    },
    {
      "id": "observerHeight",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Observer height h above water",
      "range": [10, 120, 1],
      "default": 60,
      "teaches": "The estimated distance d depends on h through the tangent of the depression angle: raising the observer steepens the sight lines and changes d even when the measured angle α stays fixed, so h must be known accurately"
    },
    {
      "id": "baselineSeparation",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Observer separation t (baseline)",
      "range": [5, 100, 1],
      "default": 40,
      "teaches": "With a larger baseline t the two sight lines converge at a wider angle, so the triangulated distance becomes better conditioned — a near-zero baseline makes d almost indeterminate, which is exactly why stereo depth degrades for distant objects"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "One observer, known height",
      "narration": "Standing at height h above the water, the observer measures the dip angle α between the horizon and the boat. Together with h this fixes a right triangle, so d follows from trigonometry alone.",
      "state": { "methodView": "Single observer (height + horizon)", "observerHeight": 60 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Height changes the estimate",
      "narration": "Drag the height slider: for the same measured angle α, a taller vantage point implies a larger distance d. The horizon reference only yields distance because h is known.",
      "state": { "methodView": "Single observer (height + horizon)", "observerHeight": 110 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two observers triangulate",
      "narration": "Switch methods: now two observers separated by a known baseline t each sight the boat, measuring angles α and β. The boat sits at the intersection of the two sight lines — no horizon reference needed.",
      "state": { "methodView": "Two observers (triangulation)", "baselineSeparation": 40 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Baseline controls reliability",
      "narration": "Shrink the separation t toward zero: the two sight lines become nearly parallel and the intersection point — hence d — becomes extremely sensitive to tiny angle errors. A wide baseline gives a well-conditioned fix, just like the separation between two eyes or cameras in stereo vision.",
      "state": { "methodView": "Two observers (triangulation)", "baselineSeparation": 10 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Distance is never seen directly — it is inferred from geometry: a known height plus an angle, or a known baseline plus two angles, and the accuracy of that inference collapses as the baseline (or height) shrinks, which is the fundamental parallax principle behind stereo vision.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Render ONE unified coastal scene (not two panels); the method toggle swaps which observer markers, sight lines, and labels are visible rather than switching panels. Left/lower area: ochre-brown cliff silhouette with a curved shoreline; right area: turquoise water up to a sharp horizontal horizon line; sky white above. Boat is a small simple sailboat icon on the water, roughly right of center. Single-observer mode: one observer circle at the cliff top, vertical 'h' line down to the water with a right-angle square at its base, a sight line to the horizon, a sight line to the boat, angle arc 'α' between them at the observer, and 'd?' labeled along the water toward the boat. Two-observer mode: two observer circles on the cliff slope connected by a baseline segment labeled 't', one sight line from each to the boat, angle arcs 'α' (upper) and 'β' (lower), a right-angle marker where relevant, and the same 'd?' label. All sight lines thin dark strokes; labels in italic serif (h, t, α, β, d?). Moving the height slider raises/lowers the observer and recomputes the sight-line geometry and displayed d; moving the baseline slider slides the lower observer along the slope and reconverges the two sight lines on the boat. Numerical readout of the estimated d should update live with both sliders."
}

